# Compatibility policy for the release bundles, read by .github/workflows/release-firmware.yml.
#
# Domesday Duplicator - LaserDisc RF sampler
# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Inns
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
#
# These are decisions, not facts, which is why they are a tracked file rather than something
# a workflow derives. The file is part of the tagged commit, so a tag pins the policy the
# same way it pins the source. Everything else in the manifest — the payload digests, the
# protocol version the firmware advertises, the register map version the gateware reports —
# is read out of the sources at release time, because those *are* facts and a workflow that
# restated them could restate them wrongly.

# The oldest capture application (gui-v* stream) permitted to install a bundle built from
# this commit — and no longer a working mechanism. Raising it will not stop a current
# application, so do not reach for it.
#
# It was compared against the application's own dotted release version. That version is
# gone: every part of a Duplicator now stamps the commit it was built from — the
# application, the FX3 firmware in its product string, the FPGA gateware in its identity
# register — and a commit orders nothing, so there is nothing left to compare a floor
# against. The field is still published because the manifest schema requires it and every
# application already in the field would fail to parse a bundle without it.
#
# What to use instead, when a bundle needs an application newer than one already fielded:
# advertise a protocol version or a register map version outside the range the old
# application knows. That refusal is enforced, it names what the old application actually
# cannot do rather than how old it is, and both numbers are read out of the sources at
# release time rather than being a decision somebody has to remember to make.
#
# Left at 0.0.0 — "no application version is too old" — which is what it has always been
# and what it should stay.
MINIMUM_APPLICATION_VERSION=0.0.0
